IQ=[('Is holiday let insurance a legal requirement in the UK?','Only in Scotland, in general. Every Scottish short-term let licence has mandatory conditions that need valid buildings insurance for the length of the licence and valid public liability insurance for each let. In England, Wales and Northern Ireland we found no general law that makes holiday let insurance compulsory, but official guidance says you should have cover suitable for short-term letting. Employers\' liability insurance is a legal requirement in every nation if you employ staff, unless an exemption applies.'),
    ('What insurance do I need for a short-term let licence in Scotland?','Valid buildings insurance for the duration of the licence and valid public liability insurance for the duration of each short-term let agreement (Schedule 3, paragraph 15 of the Short-term Lets Licensing Order 2022). Scottish Government guidance says the buildings insurance must cover the short-term let activity, but it can be your own policy, a shared policy for the building (for example an apartment block) or insurance taken out by the owner. Your council sets the evidence it wants with the application.'),
    ('Will my normal home insurance cover paying guests?','Do not assume it does. Check your policy wording and tell your insurer before your first guest. GOV.UK says hosts in England should have buildings and contents cover suitable for short-term letting, and the Association of British Insurers says not telling your insurer about changes could affect your cover. In Scotland, the buildings insurance must cover the short-term let activity.'),
    ('Is Airbnb AirCover the same as insurance?','Partly. Airbnb says the Host liability insurance part of AirCover for Hosts is an insurance policy (for UK hosts, underwritten by Zurich), with cover shown as $1 million USD. But Airbnb also says Host damage protection is not insurance or a financial service, and that AirCover is not a substitute for personal insurance. It protects you while you are hosting an Airbnb stay, so check what covers bookings you take elsewhere.'),
    ('Do I need employers\' liability insurance for my cleaner or housekeeper?','If they are your employee, usually yes. GOV.UK says you must get employers\' liability insurance as soon as you become an employer, covering at least £5 million from an authorised insurer, and you can be fined £2,500 for every day you are not properly insured. There are exemptions, for example if you only employ close family members. In Northern Ireland, Tourism NI says employers\' liability insurance is required by law once you employ someone. Whether someone counts as your employee depends on the facts, so check the HSE guide or ask an insurer or adviser.'),
    ('Do I need to tell my mortgage lender I am running a holiday let?','Check your mortgage terms before you let. Tourism NI\'s guidance says residential mortgages generally prohibit sub-letting without the lender\'s consent, that a change could mean a higher rate, a penalty or a repayment demand, and that many buy-to-let mortgages also prohibit short-term letting. It also says to check your lease or title deeds and any management company rules. That guidance is written for Northern Ireland, but reading your mortgage offer and asking your lender is a sensible check anywhere in the UK.'),
    ('Is public liability insurance required for holiday lets in Wales?','Not under a general law yet, as far as we found. The Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026 sets an insurance standard for its visitor accommodation licensing scheme: a public liability insurance policy covering liabilities to third parties. GOV.WALES says the legal default is that the licensing scheme starts in March 2030, with consultation on the regulations first. Registration with the Welsh Revenue Authority by 31 March 2027 is a separate requirement.'),
    ('Does Tourism NI require insurance for a certificate in Northern Ireland?','No. Tourism NI says public liability insurance is not required by law for most businesses, is not included in the Tourism (Northern Ireland) Order 1992 and is not part of the inspection criteria. It still recommends public liability insurance for every category of accommodation, including self-catering, on top of your existing house insurance.'),
    ('How much public liability cover do I need?','The Scottish licence condition does not set a minimum amount. Tourism NI says cover is usually for £2 million to £5 million. The Welsh Act lets Welsh Ministers set the extent of cover in regulations later. Ask your insurer or a broker what suits your let.')]
